{%- comment -%}
  The one place a composed product card decides *which* option it is standing
  on and *which variant* it therefore prices, adds and links to.

  **Three blocks need the same answer and cannot share a variable.** A theme
  block is an isolated render scope — `blocks/product-card-price`,
  `-add` and `-variants` each start from nothing but `closest.product`. In
  `snippets/product-card.liquid` all three live in one Liquid scope, so the
  swatch loop works the variant out once and the price and the button read it.
  Split into blocks, that loop would have to be written three times, and three
  copies of "the dearest available variant in the chosen metal" is three
  chances for the figure on the card to stop agreeing with what the button
  posts. That is the exact trap CLAUDE.md records under "Product card": one
  price shown, another in the bag.

  A snippet cannot hand a value back, so it **prints** and the caller captures
  and splits — the same shape as `metal-order.liquid` and `space-step.liquid`.

      {%- capture card_option_raw -%}
        {% render 'card-option', product: product, source: block.settings.option %}
      {%- endcapture -%}
      {%- assign card_option = card_option_raw | strip | split: '||' -%}
      {%- assign opt_index = card_option[0] | plus: 0 -%}

  Prints `index||value||variant_id`:

    index      the option's position in `product.options_with_values`, or -1
               where the card stands on no option at all.
    value      the value that position currently carries, or empty.
    variant_id the variant the card prices and adds.

  **The variant is the dearest available one carrying that value**, which is
  the rule already recorded for the card: the card offers no karat or size
  control, so adding has to choose, and it chooses the dearest — available
  first, because a sold-out variant cannot be bought. Where the whole value is
  sold out the dearest overall stands in and the button says Sold out.

  **Every card block calls it with the default, and that is a constraint rather
  than a habit.** A block cannot read its parent's settings and cannot read a
  sibling's, so there is no place a merchant-set "which option" could live that
  the price, the button and the control would all see. Nominating the option on
  `product-card-variants` alone would let the row mark one value selected while
  the price beside it quoted a variant chosen on another — with nothing on
  screen to say why. So the option is **derived**, identically, everywhere, and
  `product-card-variants` offers no option picker. That is the same rule the
  quick view's axes are written to: "Which control an axis gets is derived, not
  tabled."

  The parameter stays for a caller outside the card that genuinely knows which
  option it means and renders the whole card itself.

  Accepts:
    product - product object.
    source  - 'auto' (default) or a 1-based option position as a string.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign opt_index = -1
  assign opt = blank

  comment
    A nominated position is taken as given. `auto` is the design's own rule for
    the card — the metal, matched on the option's name — because "Colour is
    chosen on the card itself: the name, the price and what goes in the bag all
    follow it." Matching on the name rather than on a table of metals is the
    same rule `metal-swatch.liquid` is written to.
  endcomment
  if source != blank and source != 'auto'
    assign want = source | plus: 0 | minus: 1
    for option in product.options_with_values
      if forloop.index0 == want
        assign opt = option
        assign opt_index = want
        break
      endif
    endfor
  else
    for option in product.options_with_values
      assign option_down = option.name | downcase
      if option_down contains 'metal'
        assign opt = option
        assign opt_index = forloop.index0
        break
      endif
    endfor
  endif

  if opt.values.size < 1
    assign opt = blank
    assign opt_index = -1
  endif

  assign current = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign value = ''
  if opt_index >= 0
    assign value = current.options[opt_index]
  endif

  comment
    Dearest available, then dearest overall. Where the card stands on no option
    the whole variant list is the group, which is the answer for a piece with
    nothing to choose.
  endcomment
  assign chosen = blank
  assign dearest_any = blank
  for variant in product.variants
    assign in_group = true
    if opt_index >= 0
      unless variant.options[opt_index] == value
        assign in_group = false
      endunless
    endif

    if in_group
      if dearest_any == blank or variant.price > dearest_any.price
        assign dearest_any = variant
      endif
      if variant.available
        if chosen == blank or variant.price > chosen.price
          assign chosen = variant
        endif
      endif
    endif
  endfor

  if chosen == blank
    assign chosen = dearest_any
  endif
-%}
{{- opt_index }}||{{ value }}||{{ chosen.id -}}
